    <edge source="Investor Concerns" target="Market Selloff">
      <data key="d7">165.0</data>
      <data key="d8">The relation (Investor Concerns, Market Selloff) describes a causal link where investor anxiety directly triggers a broad decline in asset prices. The primary drivers of these concerns are rising interest rates and regulatory uncertainty. These two factors are consistently identified as the root cause, with descriptions using terms such as "primary cause," "directly caused," "causing," "triggered," and "primary drivers" to establish the connection. The selloff is presented as an ongoing or recent event, with the relationship being described in both present and past tense across the provided descriptions.

Investor concerns over rising interest rates and regulatory uncertainty are the primary cause of the market selloff. Investor concerns over interest rates and regulations are also cited as a primary cause. The relationship is described as both a direct cause and a driving force behind the selloff, indicating a strong and consistent causal link between investor anxiety and market declines.&lt;SEP&gt;Investor concerns over rising interest rates and regulatory uncertainty caused the market selloff.&lt;SEP&gt;Investor concerns over rising interest rates and regulatory uncertainty are the primary cause of the market selloff.&lt;SEP&gt;Investor concerns over rising interest rates and regulatory uncertainty are causing the market selloff.&lt;SEP&gt;Investor concerns over rising interest rates and regulatory uncertainty are a primary cause of the market selloff.</data>
