    <edge source="Supply Constraints" target="Crude Oil">
      <data key="d7">106.0</data>
      <data key="d8">The relation (Crude Oil, Supply Constraints) describes a fundamental dynamic in the global oil market where limitations on the availability of crude oil act as a primary driver of upward price movements. These supply constraints are consistently identified as a key factor that supports and sustains rallies in crude oil prices. The descriptions emphasize that such constraints are not merely a background condition but an active and significant force underpinning price increases. Whether characterized as a general support or a specific key factor, the core assertion remains that restricted supply directly contributes to the elevation and strengthening of crude oil market values.

This relationship highlights the sensitivity of crude oil pricing to disruptions or limitations in production, distribution, or geopolitical access, reinforcing the idea that any reduction in available supply tends to push prices higher. The consistent repetition across multiple descriptions underscores the widely accepted view that supply constraints are a critical, ongoing factor supporting the rise and rally in crude oil prices.</data>
      <data key="d9">causation,cause and effect,market condition,market driver,market dynamics,market factor,price driver,price influence,price support,supply factor</data>
